   CSS NOTES: Calculating Specificity

   Specificity Levels (High → Low):
   1. Inline styles
   2. ID selectors (#id)
   3. Class selectors, attributes, pseudo-classes
   4. Element selectors, pseudo-elements

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Inline Styles
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   - Written inside HTML using style=""
   - Highest priority
   - Overrides all selectors

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   ID Selectors
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   - Each #id increases ID specificity
   - Beats class and element selectors

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Class-Level Selectors
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Includes:
   - Classes (.box)
   - Attributes ([type="text"])
   - Pseudo-classes (:hover, :focus, :checked)

   All are counted at the same level.

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Element-Level Selectors
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Includes:
   - HTML elements (p, div, li)
   - Pseudo-elements (::before, ::first-line)

   Lowest priority level.

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Comparison Rules
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   - Specificity is compared level by level
   - Higher level wins immediately
   - Order matters only if specificity is equal

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Does NOT Affect Specificity
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   - Universal selector (*)
   - Combinators (>, +, ~, space)
   - Selector length or nesting

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Golden Rule
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   More precise selector = higher specificity

